= Appendix I — Comparisons

== I.1 Health and environment
*Hazard versus exposure:* potential to cause harm versus contact with it.  
*Risk versus diagnosis:* changed probability versus an individual clinical conclusion.  
*Sanitation versus hygiene:* safe excreta management versus practices such as handwashing; both matter.

== I.2 Disease and prevention
*Pathogen versus vector:* disease-causing agent versus organism transmitting an agent.  
*Malaria versus TB:* parasite commonly transmitted by mosquitoes versus bacterial infection that can spread through air.  
*Antibiotic versus vaccine:* medicine acting against susceptible bacteria versus a preparation training immunity against a particular agent.

== I.3 Safety and relationships
*Bleeding versus burn:* firm direct pressure when appropriate versus cooling a thermal burn with cool running water.  
*Pregnancy prevention versus STI protection:* distinct questions; many contraceptive methods do not protect against STIs.  
*Consent versus pressure:* freely given specific agreement that can be withdrawn versus coercion, which does not establish consent.
